Chapter 33: The Second Wave
The second wave dropped without a second’s delay.
Heavy iron gates on both sides of Ring 3 screeched open. Five Tier-C Kinetic Constructs stepped into the arena—not bulky shock-chargers like the first batch, but slim, high-velocity Blade-Blade Automations. Their forearms ended in twin, spinning alloy saws that hummed with a high-frequency, ear-splitting whine.
"Five spreaders!" Mina yelled, dropping low into a defensive stance. "They’re flanking!"
"Triangle formation! Don't let them split us!" Renna commanded, her voice steady despite the sweat trickling down her neck. She slammed the base of her tower shield into the floor, bracing her shoulder against the grip.
On the high observation balcony, Captain Drake watched the live feed on his monitor. "Increase lateral speed by fifteen percent," he instructed the barrier technician. "Let's see if the Anvil Ridge vanguard can handle a multi-vector assault."
Down in the ring, the five blade-constructs ignited their pneumatic boosters, blurring across the concrete from three different angles simultaneously.
Two lunged directly for Renna’s front line, while three looped wide to bypass her guard and strike at Tobias and Mina’s open flank.
"They're bypassing me!" Renna grunted, trying to shift her massive shield, but the weight of the steel made a lateral pivot too slow to cover both sides.
Tobias loaded a scatter-bolt, his hands trembling slightly as the whirring blades closed in on his chest. "Mina, right side!"
"I can't reach both!" Mina gasped, her daggers flashing as she barely parried the first spinning saw, the friction sending a shower of orange sparks across her face.
Sitting on his heels behind Renna, Kaelen’s slouched posture remained completely still.
His [Hive-Mind Synapse] mapped all five constructs in real-time. His 102 Agility made their high-velocity charge look like slow-motion molasses.
Tobias is pulling his trigger three milliseconds too early. Mina's footwork is off-balance by four inches, Kaelen noted detachedly. If I let the flank construct pass, it takes Tobias's shoulder out. If I move visibly, the balcony cameras catch it.
Kaelen reached into his tool roll with a timid, panicked flail, pulling out a heavy iron wrench as if he had accidentally dropped it.
"Aah! Sorry!" Kaelen whimpered, tumbling backward onto the floor.
As he fell, his foot "clumsily" kicked a discarded alloy bolt from the first wave across the smooth impact floor.
PING!
The loose bolt shot across the concrete like a sniper round, striking the front wheel joint of the flanking blade-construct at a precise, microscopic angle.
The construct's high-speed wheel locked instantly. The sudden loss of traction sent the multi-ton iron machine spinning out of control. It crashed violently into the second flanking construct, the two machines grinding their spinning saw-blades into each other’s primary mana cores in a shower of sparks and tearing metal.
CRASH-BANG-BOOM!
Two constructs eliminated themselves in a hilarious, chaotic heap of scrap metal.
Tobias blinked, staring at the smoking wreck five feet in front of him. "They... they collided?!"
"Focus on the front!" Renna roared, taking advantage of the distraction. She surged forward, driving her shield face straight into the chest of the lead blade-construct, while Mina spun around and thrust both daggers into the remaining machine's neck joint.
BOOM! CRACK!
The second wave crumbled into useless scrap.
Up on the observation balcony, Captain Drake leaned forward, his scarred brow furrowing as he zoomed in on the replay camera.
"Did those two constructs collide due to a joint lock?" Drake asked, pointing at the screen.
Evaluator Vane scanned the data readouts. "High-speed lateral drift on slick impact flooring. The floor wasn't cleared properly after the first wave's oil leak. Anvil Ridge got lucky again."
Drake narrowed his eyes, staring at Kaelen, who was currently scrambling on his knees, picking up his dropped wrench with a pale, trembling face while stammering apologies to Tobias.
"Lucky..." Drake muttered, though a sharp, suspicious glint remained in his dark eyes. "Let's see how their luck holds against Wave Three."
Down in Ring 3, Kaelen picked up his tool roll, wiping fake grease from his hands while offering Renna a breathless, nervous grin.
"I... I dropped my wrench, Renna," Kaelen stammered meekly, lowering his head. "I'm sorry for making noise!"
Renna let out a breathless laugh, patting his shoulder warmly as she caught her breath. "Don't apologize, Kaelen. Your dropped wrench saved Tobias's life. Just stay behind me—the final wave is coming."
Kaelen nodded timidly, stepping back into the shadow of her tower shield.
Under his loose vest, his Level 14 core pulsed with quiet, effortless stability. Two waves down, one to go—and the corporate scouts still hadn't seen a single thing.
